\section{Minimum Registers for an Expression Tree}

Suppose the leaves of a binary expression tree are independent loads, each
internal operation reads two registers and can overwrite one of them, and
only the root result is needed outside the tree. For now, assume evaluation
order is unobservable, there are no memory operands, and spilling and
recomputation are forbidden. The Sethi--Ullman algorithm, discussed in
Cooper and Torczon's \emph{Engineering a Compiler}, labels subtrees by their
minimum register requirement. A leaf requires one register. If the
left and right children require $a$ and $b$ respectively, completing the
left child first requires $\max(a,1+b)$ registers: the second child must
run while the first result is still live. The reverse order requires
$\max(b,1+a)$. Thus the label is
\[
  N(t)=
  \begin{cases}
     1 & t\text{ is a leaf},\\
     a+1 & a=b,\\
     \max(a,b) & a\ne b.
  \end{cases}
\]
The recurrence is also a lower bound if evaluation can interleave the
children. Each child needs its own label even in isolation. If the labels
are equal, the first child to begin keeps at least one intermediate or
final result live until the parent executes: discarding its entire
frontier would require recomputation. The other child must reach its own
peak while this register is occupied. A bottom-up labeling pass and a
top-down emission pass therefore suffice in this model.

For $a-((b+c)(d+e))$, each sum has label two, the product has label
three, and the root has label three. Computing $a$ first would require
four registers. Compute the product first, load $a$ second, and then
subtract the product \emph{from} $a$. Reversing evaluation order is not
the same as reversing operand positions. Forced source evaluation order
can invalidate the minimum; if $a$ is an effectful operation that must
precede the product, the three-register schedule is no longer justified.

\section{Tree Rules and Shared Values}

With two registers, the product in the example can be computed by
storing the first sum, computing the second, then reloading the first
sum into the freed register. This uses one store and one reload. A target
that can multiply a register by a stack operand may eliminate the
separate reload. Aho and Johnson's \emph{Optimal Code Generation for
Expression Trees} develops cost-based tree code generation: the state
must distinguish the forms of result that a parent instruction can
actually consume. A rule admitting a memory operand and a rule admitting
an immediate are different choices, not two estimates for the same tree
label.

The independence used in the recurrence disappears on an expression
DAG. In $(x+y)(x+y)$, sharing the sum and reading its register twice
avoids the label of three for two independent sums. Conversely, a
shared value with consumers far apart in the schedule can force a long
live range. Sethi's \emph{Complete Register Allocation Problems}
distinguishes models in which each value is computed once from models
that allow recomputation; the tree recurrence does not establish an
efficient solution to either general straight-line problem.

\section{Next Use within a Fixed Block}

Liberatore, Farach-Colton, and Kremer's \emph{Evaluation of Algorithms
for Local Register Allocation} separates the boundary assignment from
local allocation and physical register assignment. Given a fixed
instruction order and a boundary contract, a backward scan records
the next use of each value, including any required delivery at block
exit. The forward allocator pins all inputs of the current instruction
until their reads, and evicts a value when an unpinned register is
needed. A dead value needs no store. A clean live value with a current
memory home needs only a future reload; a dirty live value without one
must be saved unless it can be legally regenerated. Furthest next use
is a useful heuristic, not a guarantee when these costs differ.

For example, enter with dirty $a$ in one register and clean $b$ in a
second. Load $c$, read $(a,c)$, and then read $b$, with no live-out
values and no clobbers at the reads. Evicting $b$ permits the load of
$c$ without a store; $b$ then reloads into a register freed by the
first read. If instead $b$ is dirty, the same trace requires a save
before the load, even though none of the three values is live-out.
The invariant to check after each operation is that every needed value
has a valid register, a current stack copy, or a valid recipe; the
exit check additionally verifies the required destination locations.
The interval allocator in Chapter~\ref{ch:ext-233} makes related
decisions across block boundaries.
